224| \begin{definition}[Metallocenes]\label{def:b3:organometallic-bonding:metallocene}
225| A \emph{sandwich compound}\index{sandwich compound} has a metal between two
226| parallel planar ring ligands bonded through all their carbons; a
227| \emph{metallocene}\index{metallocene} is a sandwich compound with two $\eta^5$-cyclopentadienyl
228| rings, $\ce{M(C5H5)2}$.
229| \end{definition}
230| 
231| \begin{proposition}[The frontier orbitals of ferrocene]\label{prop:b3:organometallic-bonding:ferrocene}
232| In a metallocene with parallel rings ($D_{5d}$), the metal $d$ orbitals split into $e_{2g}$
233| ($d_{xy}$, $d_{x^2-y^2}$) and $a_{1g}$ ($d_{z^2}$), nearly non-bonding and close together, and $e_{1g}^\ast$ ($d_{xz}$,
234| $d_{yz}$), strongly antibonding; ferrocene, with six $d$ electrons in $e_{2g}^4a_{1g}^2$, has 18
235| electrons and no unpaired electron.
236| \end{proposition}
237| 
238| \begin{proof}[Argued]
239| The $\pi$ orbitals of the two rings combine into symmetry-adapted pairs $a_{1g}$, $a_{2u}$,
240| $e_{1g}$, $e_{1u}$, $e_{2g}$, $e_{2u}$ (from the five $\pi$ orbitals of each ring, with zero, one and two
241| nodal planes through the axis). The filled ring $e_{1g}$ combination overlaps
242| strongly with $d_{xz}$, $d_{yz}$ (one nodal plane through the axis each): they form bonding
243| orbitals, mostly ring, and antibonding $e_{1g}^\ast$, mostly metal, pushed high. $d_{xy}$, $d_{x^2-y^2}$
244| (two nodal planes) meet only the empty ring $e_{2g}$ orbitals and are slightly
245| stabilised by back-donation; $d_{z^2}$ points at the hole in the middle of each ring and
246| hardly overlaps. Six electrons fill $e_{2g}$ and $a_{1g}$; with the twelve electrons of the
247| bonding ring combinations, the count is 18.
248| \end{proof}
